  "id": "Compound::DB01595 - Gene::2566",
  "verbalization": "Nitrazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrd, Gabrq, and Gabrp, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These receptors are critical for the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba-a receptor activity is a well-documented mechanism of action. The evidence suggests that Nitrazepam exerts its effects by interacting with Gaba-a receptor subunits, which are involved in the regulation of synaptic transmission and neuronal excitation. The shared involvement of these subunits in Gaba-a receptor activity and their subsequent participation in the Gaba-a receptor activity pathway points to a common mechanism by which Nitrazepam influences Gaba-a receptor function. The integrated evidence supports a direct relationship between Nitrazepam and Gabrg2, as it is the final participant in the Gaba-a receptor activity pathway, indicating that Nitrazepam may modulate Gabrg2 through its interaction with Gaba-a receptor subunits. The consistent binding sites across different subunits suggest a general mechanism of action that is relevant to the function of Gaba-a receptors, thereby supporting the proposed relationship between Nitrazepam and Gabrg2.",
